# Reference cron lines for a Linux production host - NOT active anywhere,
# just documents the same schedule scripts/setup_windows_tasks.ps1 registers
# locally on Windows, translated to cron. Wire these into the production
# server's actual crontab (`crontab -e`) once that server exists; adjust the
# path if the project doesn't live at /opt/acts-importer there.
#
# Both jobs log their own output already (see run_monthly.ps1 /
# run_quarterly_refresh.ps1's Windows equivalents below) - give them
# equivalent shell wrappers if you want the exact same log files on Linux,
# or just redirect stdout/stderr from cron directly as shown here.

# `timeout 4h` mirrors the 4-hour ExecutionTimeLimit set on the Windows
# tasks - the import has retry-with-backoff for ordinary transient failures,
# but a genuine hang inside one blocking HTTP call (confirmed live, past its
# own 30s timeout) can't retry its way out. Without a hard cap, cron just
# lets it sit forever unattended. Safe either way since imports are
# idempotent - the next scheduled run resumes cleanly.

# Monthly, 1st of every month at 02:00 - picks up newly published acts only.
0 2 1 * * cd /opt/acts-importer && timeout 4h ./venv/bin/python manage.py import_acts >> logs/monthly.log 2>&1

# Quarterly, 1st of Jan/Apr/Jul/Oct at 03:00 - full --refresh, re-syncs
# acts already stored too (catches amendments/repeals the monthly run misses).
0 3 1 1,4,7,10 * cd /opt/acts-importer && timeout 4h ./venv/bin/python manage.py import_acts --refresh >> logs/quarterly_refresh.log 2>&1
